## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates practical API boundary design, basic rate limiting, and graceful degradation in a beginner-friendly Azure setup.

## Key Concepts
- API Management quotas and rate limits by user
- Per-user spend cap and simple state store
- Fallback to rule-based replies on failure
- Observability for quotas and failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal mock of request flow with quota check and fallback
async function handle(req) {
  const user = req.userId;
  const quota = getUserQuota(user);
  if (quota.remaining <= 0) return cannedReply();
  try {
    const res = await callOpenAI(req.payload);
    debitQuota(user, req.payload.length);
    return res;
  } catch {
    return cannedReply();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota expiry and fallback paths?
- How would you simulate OpenAI downtime in a local test environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect a governance-heavy, production-ready OpenAI integration across tenants, including data redaction, KB grounding, per-tenant state, and actionable remediation.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI moderation and content safety
- Text Analytics for redaction
- Cognitive Search KB grounding with versioning
- Cosmos DB per-tenant state
- Azure Boards for remediation/workflow
- Canary deployments and end-to-end auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-pipeline orchestration sketch
async function evaluate(tp) {
  const moderated = await mod.check(tp.prompt, tp.response);
  const redacted = redactor.redact(moderated);
  const grounded = kb.ground(redacted, tp.tenantId);
  const risk = riskStore.update(tp.tenantId, grounded.score);
  board.createTask(tp.tenantId, risk, grounded.details);
